# Performance Rules

## Next.js Optimization

### Server Components (Default)

```typescript
// TEACHING: Server Components reduce client JS bundle
// Data fetching happens on server, HTML is sent to client

// GOOD - Server Component fetches data
export default async function EventsPage() {
  // This runs on server, no client JS needed
  const events = await db.select().from(event);
  return <EventList events={events} />;
}

// TEACHING: Only add "use client" when you NEED client features
// Each "use client" adds to JS bundle
```

### Dynamic Imports

```typescript
// TEACHING: Lazy load heavy components to reduce initial bundle

import dynamic from "next/dynamic";

// TEACHING: This component won't load until it's needed
const HeavyChart = dynamic(() => import("@/components/chart"), {
  loading: () => <Skeleton className="h-64" />,
  ssr: false, // Skip server rendering if component needs browser APIs
});
```

### Image Optimization

```typescript
// TEACHING: Always use next/image for automatic optimization
// It handles lazy loading, responsive sizes, and modern formats

import Image from "next/image";

// GOOD - Optimized
<Image
  src="/hero.jpg"
  alt="Hero image"
  width={1200}
  height={600}
  priority // Add for above-the-fold images
/>

// BAD - No optimization
<img src="/hero.jpg" alt="Hero image" />
```

## Database Performance

```typescript
// TEACHING: N+1 queries are the most common performance killer
// Fetch related data in ONE query, not loops

// BAD - N+1 problem (1 query + N queries for each event)
const events = await db.select().from(event);
for (const e of events) {
  e.organizer = await db.select().from(user).where(eq(user.id, e.organizerId));
}

// GOOD - Single query with join
const events = await db.query.event.findMany({
  with: {
    organizer: true, // Drizzle handles the join
  },
});

// TEACHING: Always add indexes for frequently queried columns
// Check slow queries with: EXPLAIN ANALYZE <query>
```

## React Performance

```typescript
// TEACHING: React Compiler handles most optimizations automatically
// But these patterns still matter:

// 1. Keep state as local as possible
// BAD - State too high, re-renders everything
function App() {
  const [searchQuery, setSearchQuery] = useState("");
  return (
    <div>
      <SearchInput value={searchQuery} onChange={setSearchQuery} />
      <ExpensiveComponent /> {/* Re-renders on every keystroke! */}
    </div>
  );
}

// GOOD - State colocated with usage
function SearchSection() {
  const [searchQuery, setSearchQuery] = useState("");
  return <SearchInput value={searchQuery} onChange={setSearchQuery} />;
}

function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <SearchSection />
      <ExpensiveComponent /> {/* Only renders once */}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Caching Strategies

```typescript
// TEACHING: Cache expensive operations appropriately

// Next.js fetch caching (server)
const data = await fetch(url, {
  next: { revalidate: 3600 }, // Cache for 1 hour
});

// Redis caching (via Upstash)
import { redis } from "@/server/realtime/redis";

async function getCachedData(key: string) {
  const cached = await redis.get(key);
  if (cached) return cached;

  const fresh = await expensiveOperation();
  await redis.set(key, fresh, { ex: 3600 }); // Expire in 1 hour
  return fresh;
}
```

## Measuring Performance

1. **Lighthouse** - Overall web vitals
2. **React DevTools Profiler** - Component render times
3. **Network tab** - Bundle sizes, request waterfalls
4. **`console.time()`** - Quick timing measurements
5. **Database EXPLAIN** - Query analysis
